{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte uses a compiler to build your code which optimises rendering. By default, variables will run once, unless they are referenced in your markup. To make a different variable or function reactive, you need to use "},{"type":"link","href":"https://svelte.dev/tutorial/reactive-declarations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reactive declarations"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This also applies to Svelte Query."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the below example, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchInterval"},{"type":"text","value":" option is set from the variable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"intervalMs"},{"type":"text","value":", which is edited by the input field. However, as the query is not told it should react to changes in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"intervalMs"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetchInterval"},{"type":"text","value":" will not change when the input value changes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  const query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, you can prefix the query with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$: "},{"type":"text","value":" to tell the compiler it should be reactive."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  $: query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />","lang":"svelte"}],"headings":[]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nSvelte uses a compiler to build your code which optimises rendering. By default, variables will run once, unless they are referenced in your markup. To make a different variable or function reactive, you need to use [reactive declarations](https://svelte.dev/tutorial/reactive-declarations). This also applies to Svelte Query.\n\nIn the below example, the `refetchInterval` option is set from the variable `intervalMs`, which is edited by the input field. However, as the query is not told it should react to changes in `intervalMs`, `refetchInterval` will not change when the input value changes.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  const query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />\n```\n\nTo solve this, you can prefix the query with `$: ` to tell the compiler it should be reactive.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  $: query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: reactivity\ntitle: Reactivity\n---\n\nSvelte uses a compiler to build your code which optimises rendering. By default, variables will run once, unless they are referenced in your markup. To make a different variable or function reactive, you need to use [reactive declarations](https://svelte.dev/tutorial/reactive-declarations). This also applies to Svelte Query.\n\nIn the below example, the `refetchInterval` option is set from the variable `intervalMs`, which is edited by the input field. However, as the query is not told it should react to changes in `intervalMs`, `refetchInterval` will not change when the input value changes.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  const query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />\n```\n\nTo solve this, you can prefix the query with `$: ` to tell the compiler it should be reactive.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createQuery } from '@tanstack/svelte-query'\n\n  let intervalMs = 1000\n\n  const endpoint = 'http://localhost:5173/api/data'\n\n  $: query = createQuery({\n    queryKey: ['refetch'],\n    queryFn: async () => await fetch(endpoint).then((r) => r.json()),\n    refetchInterval: intervalMs,\n  })\n</script>\n\n<input bind:value={intervalMs} type=\"number\" />\n```\n"}
